diff --git a/DOCS/man/vf.rst b/DOCS/man/vf.rst
index a1f036a..14167ef 100644
--- a/DOCS/man/vf.rst
+++ b/DOCS/man/vf.rst
@@ -937,3 +937,56 @@ Available mpv-only filters are:
         Do not use this with ``--vo=gpu``. It will apply filtering twice, since
         most ``--vo=gpu`` options are unconditionally applied to the ``gpu``
         filter. There is no mechanism in mpv to prevent this.
+
+``motionblade=...``
+    Motion-compensated frame interpolation on the GPU with Vulkan. New frames
+    are computed between the source frames, so motion looks smoother on
+    displays with a higher refresh rate than the video.
+
+    With VA-API or Vulkan hardware decoding the frames stay on the GPU; other
+    frames are uploaded from system memory. Supported formats are 8 to 16 bit
+    YUV with 4:2:0, 4:2:2 or 4:4:4 chroma, planar or semi-planar (``nv12``,
+    ``p010``). Other formats and frames smaller than 64x64 pass through
+    unchanged, as does everything when no suitable Vulkan device is found.
+
+    The filter holds one source frame back, so it can see where objects that
+    are hidden in one frame move next. Audio stays in sync.
+
+    Sub-options:
+
+    ``factor=<auto|2-8>``
+        A number multiplies the frame rate by that factor (default: 2). The
+        output never exceeds the display refresh rate: a factor that would
+        exceed it is lowered, and videos at the refresh rate pass through.
+        ``auto`` outputs one frame per display refresh, using the display
+        refresh rate or ``--display-fps-override``. Videos with a frame rate at
+        or above the refresh rate pass through.
+
+    ``max-fps=<0-1000>``
+        Do not interpolate videos above this frame rate (default: 0, no limit).
+
+    ``max-gap=<0-10>``
+        Do not interpolate between frames that are more than this many seconds
+        apart (default: 0.25, 0 means no limit).
+
+    ``pulldown=<auto|no>``
+        Film converted to 29.97 fps for TV or DVD (3:2 pulldown) repeats some
+        fields, so motion stutters. With ``auto`` (the default) the filter
+        finds this pattern and restores the original 23.976 fps frames before
+        interpolating, using libavfilter's ``fieldmatch``, ``yadif`` and
+        ``decimate``. It works on frames in system memory only (software
+        decoding or ``--hwdec=auto-copy``). Leave ``--deinterlace`` off: it
+        runs before this filter and hides the pattern.
+
+    ``zerocopy=<auto|drm|vulkan|no>``
+        Which paths may keep frames on the GPU: VA-API decoding through dma-buf
+        (``drm``), Vulkan decoding on the player's device (``vulkan``), both
+        (``auto``, the default) or none (``no``, every frame is copied through
+        system memory; use this if a driver shows wrong colours or glitches).
+
+    The factor can be changed during playback with
+    ``vf-command all factor <2-8|auto|off|cycle>``; ``cycle`` steps through
+    2, 4, 8, auto and off. The current value is readable as
+    ``vf-metadata/<label>/factor``.
+
+    Example: ``--vf=motionblade --hwdec=auto``
